    "narrative": "For today's matchup, our core offensive engine is centered on **Junior Caminero** (78.48), **Yordan Alvarez** (78.37), and **Trea Turner** (77.64). **Junior Caminero** represents our strongest play, bolstered by a significant +14% park factor adjustment and a favorable matchup against Gabriel Hughes. Meanwhile, **Yordan Alvarez** continues to operate at an elite level, with his 0.476 xwOBA driving a high-ceiling performance despite a slight park penalty. **Trea Turner** and **Kyle Schwarber** are both prime targets against Zack Littell; while we acknowledge the -4% hit to **Trea Turner**\u2019s SB efficiency due to a high-hold opposing battery, their respective +19% SP skill matchups provide more than enough cushion to anchor our daily output.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved the exclusion of **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (46.67), who nearly cracked the lineup. While his leadoff projection and park factors were appealing, the optimizer ultimately pivoted to **Lars Nootbaar** (65.16) and **Josh Smith** (56.02) to maximize dynamic platoon advantages against right-handed pitching. **Christian Yelich** (57.48) also warrants close observation; while he faces a formidable opponent in Jared Jones (3.45 xERA), his +13% platoon boost makes him a necessary piece for stabilization against high-velocity righties.\n\nRisks today are concentrated in our catching and middle infield spots. **Gary Sanchez** (41.40) and **Chase Meidroth** (49.31) are firmly in the marginal tier, largely because of the \"Lineup Pending\" status and unfavorable metrics against Jared Jones and Patrick Sandoval, respectively. We must prioritize efficiency here as we navigate potential lineup shifts. Ensure that if official orders deviate significantly from our assumptions for **Gary Sanchez** or **Colt Keith** (50.95), we remain ready to pivot to bench depth like **Carson Benge** (48.18) if the situation demands a safer floor to preserve our limited game caps.",
